{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_i4lla3dhmcboyc4mdamekrmgcahzukf3ras2yoobr2lg4yefb4ya","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. **The Rust translation is correct — no divergences found, and no fixes to `src/lib.rs` were needed.**\n\n## Completion gate\n\n| Gate | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` — 0 missing/undefined non-libc symbols | ✅ 1:1 (`wcscat`), diff empty |\n| Phase B — every `CONFIGS.md` row passes randomized inputs | ✅ 18/18 rows |\n| Binary stdout comparison | N/A — no `add_executable`, no `[[bin]]` |\n| Phase C — every `ERRORS.md` row has a passing test | ✅ 17/17 (E1–E11, G1–G6) |\n| Holds under every feature combination | ✅ crate declares no `[features]`; default / `--no-default-features` / `--all-features` all pass |\n\n39 tests total, all green; also cross-checked under all four C(`-O0`/`-O2`) × Rust(debug/release) builds, which matters because rows G4/G5 exercise pointer-arithmetic overflow that is UB in C.\n\n## Two harness bugs I found and fixed — both would have faked a pass\n\n1. **`cargo test` never builds a `cdylib`.** The suite was dlopening a **stale** `.so` from an earlier `cargo build --release`. I proved this by mutating `34 → 33` in the Rust: the suite still reported 35/35 passing. Fixed with `run_tests.sh` (builds before testing) plus an mtime **staleness guard** in the harness that hard-fails rather than running vacuously — verified it fires.\n2. **`wcscat` collides with glibc's own `wcscat`** (different 2-arg signature). If either `dlsym` had resolved to libc, every assertion would have compared the wrong function. `tests/phase_d_provenance.rs` pins this down: distinct addresses, each inside the expected object per `/proc/self/maps`, and both return exactly `22` for `numElem == 0` (which libc's `wcscat` cannot).\n\n## Sensitivity evidence\n\nRather than trusting a green run, `mutation_check.sh` injects 16 bug classes — wrong error codes, dropped `dst[0]=0` side effects, reordered validation, off-by-one window, unsigned `wchar_t`, `wchar_t` as `u16`, zero-padding, added NUL-termination \"fix\" — and **all 16 are detected**. Two of my initial mutants were *not* caught; I model-checked them exhaustively in Python and confirmed both were **equivalent mutants** (no observable divergence — one only added a redundant OOB read), not test blind spots, then replaced them with genuinely divergent versions.\n\nBehaviours worth flagging as deliberately preserved bug-for-bug: on truncation the C copies part of `src` **before** clobbering `dst[0]`, so error paths are only verified by comparing the whole buffer, not the return code; and `numElem == 0` returns `22` *without* touching `dst`, whereas `src == NULL` returns `22` *and* sets `dst[0] = 0` — an ordering distinction (rows E4/E5 vs E6) that mutant M5 confirms is load-bearing."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1273,"cache_read_input_tokens":110515,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1273,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6655b827-6eeb-4f93-bd22-a553f2ee9e5c","uuid":"f665fed8-ef75-4bbf-b219-95b4cf179d58","timestamp":"2026-09-05T02:42:00.319Z"}
